.TH SASH 1 "2026-11-11" "safebash" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sash \- compile safebash programs to static Linux executables
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sash
.RI [ option " ...] " file.sb
.br
.B sash
.RI [ option " ...]"
.BI "\-c " source
.br
.B sash \-\-extract
.I binary
.RB [ source | ir | manifest ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sash
compiles safebash, a deliberately constrained, Bash-derived language.
It checks the source, generates native code and links a static ELF
executable against the safebash runtime.
It does not interpret the source or run the resulting program.
.PP
Input is a UTF-8 source file or the text supplied with
.BR \-c .
The recommended source suffix is
.BR .sb ;
the compiler does not require a particular suffix.
.PP
safebash is not a drop-in Bash replacement.
For Bash input, use the separate
.B sash-convert
tool and review its generated source and annotations before compiling.
This page describes the compiler command, not the full language.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "\-c " source
Compile the supplied source text instead of reading a file.
Quote it for the invoking shell so that shell does not expand it first.
.TP
.BI "\-o " output
Write the native executable to
.IR output .
Without this option,
.B \-c
uses
.BR a.out .
File input loses a trailing
.B .sh
or
.B .bash
suffix; other filenames have
.B .out
appended.
Thus
.B program.sb
defaults to
.BR program.sb.out .
An explicit output path is recommended.
This option does not redirect inspection or C output.
.TP
.B \-\-emit-c
Write C source to standard output instead of creating an executable.
The generated C uses the same runtime ABI as native output and must be
compiled and linked against a matching
.BR libsash_rt.a .
It is not a standalone libc-based translation.
.TP
.B \-\-emit-ir
Write the frontend IR as inert Janet data to standard output.
This is the parsed program before internal lexical-name qualification,
not lowered SSA or executable Janet code.
.TP
.B \-\-manifest
Write resolved external-command dependencies to standard output,
one absolute path per line.
The manifest does not describe libraries or further commands that those
external programs may themselves need.
.TP
.BI "\-\-extract " binary
Read an embedded artifact from an existing executable, without compiling
source or executing the binary.
An optional following selector chooses
.BR source ,
.B ir
or
.BR manifest ;
the default is
.BR source .
An invalid selector or an unreadable or missing artifact is an error.
.TP
.BI \-\-heap-size= size
Set the generated program's runtime heap reservation.
The default is
.B 256M
(256 MiB).
Use a positive decimal byte count, optionally followed by
.BR K ,
.B M
or
.B G
for powers of 1024; lowercase suffixes are also accepted.
For example:
.BR 512K ,
.BR 64M ,
.BR 2G .
This setting is compiled into native and C output, not read from the
program's environment.
It does not limit compiler memory or external-command memory.
.TP
.BI \-\-sysroot= root
Check external-command paths against a directory or a SquashFS image
instead of the build host's root.
Image filenames must end in
.B .squashfs
or
.BR .sqsh ,
and require
.B unsquashfs
on the compiler's PATH.
The selected root is not prepended to the paths embedded in the program.
This option does not select a machine architecture, target runtime or linker,
and does not run the program in a chroot.
.TP
.B \-\-trace
Include branch and loop outcome tracing in a native executable.
Enable it when running that executable with
.BR SASH_TRACE=1 .
Trace records go to standard error; this is not shell command tracing.
The C emitter does not provide this tracing.
.TP
.B \-\-no-taint
Disable both frontend and SSA taint checking.
Parsing, structural validation and type checking remain enabled.
This is an explicit trust bypass, not validation of external data.
.TP
.B \-\-no-taint-cst
Testing switch: disable frontend/AST taint checking while retaining the
SSA taint check for native and C emission.
The spelling is historical; the compiler no longer has a CST.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and the main options to standard output, then exit successfully.
.SH OUTPUT
The default operation creates a native executable and prints a completion
message to standard output.
Diagnostics go to standard error; most source diagnostics include a source
location and an
.B SBnnnn
code, while driver and I/O errors may be unnumbered.
.PP
Use one of
.BR \-\-emit-c ,
.B \-\-emit-ir
or
.B \-\-manifest
instead of native compilation.
Their payload goes to standard output; use shell redirection to save it.
.PP
IR and manifest output follow the frontend checks and lowering, but do
not run the final SSA taint check used by native and C emission.
Successful inspection is therefore not proof that executable emission
will be accepted.
Extraction is a separate artifact-reading operation.
.PP
Native executables embed the source, IR and command manifest for
.BR \-\-extract .
Embedded source is recoverable text, not encrypted or authenticated.
.SH COMMAND RESOLUTION
Bare external-command names are resolved at compile time using the
compiler process's PATH.
Only absolute PATH entries participate; relative and empty entries are
ignored.
Explicit absolute command paths are checked directly.
Candidates must be regular files with execute permission.
.PP
Resolved absolute paths are compiled into the program.
Those commands must be available at the corresponding paths when it runs.
Static linking does not bundle external commands or their dependencies.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PATH
Used for external-command resolution and to locate build-host tools:
.B ld
for native linking, and
.B unsquashfs
when inspecting an image sysroot.
.TP
.B SASH_TRACE
Read by the generated program, not a replacement for the compiler's
.B \-\-trace
option.
Set it to
.B 1
to enable tracing in a trace-enabled native binary.
An unset or empty value, or a value beginning with
.BR 0 ,
disables tracing.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I sash-rt/target/release/libsash_rt.a
Runtime archive used by the native linker.
Its path is tied to the checkout where
.B sash
was built, not the current directory or the installed compiler's directory.
Keep that build checkout and archive available.
.B SASH_RT_LIB
is not an override honored by the native driver.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The requested compilation, inspection, extraction or help operation succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
A reported usage, input, validation, compilation or linking error occurred.
.PP
Unexpected compiler failures may return another nonzero status; see BUGS.
These are compiler exit statuses, not the exit status of the generated
program, which runs separately.
.SH EXAMPLES
Compile a safebash file and run the resulting program:
.PP
.EX
sash program.sb \-o program
\&./program
.EE
.PP
Compile inline source, then run it:
.PP
.EX
sash \-c 'echo hello' \-o hello
\&./hello
.EE
.PP
Inspect source without linking, or retrieve source from a native binary:
.PP
.EX
sash \-\-manifest program.sb
sash \-\-emit-ir program.sb > program.ir
sash \-\-emit-c program.sb > program.c
sash \-\-extract program source > recovered.sb
.EE
.PP
Trace a branch in a native executable:
.PP
.EX
sash \-\-trace \-c 'int n=1; if (( n > 0 )) { echo yes }' \-o traced
SASH_TRACE=1 ./traced
.EE
.SH LIMITATIONS
The verified native platform is x86-64 Linux.
Full AArch64 support and cross-compilation are not yet delivered.
The compiler, language and runtime remain under active development.
.PP
Validation is not a sandbox or proof that an authored validation predicate
is correct.
Programs retain their operating-system permissions.
Explicit trust operations and taint-bypass switches weaken taint protection.
Compilation alone does not sign a binary or make it tamper-evident.
.PP
Regex operations stop at the first interior NUL even though scalar strings
retain later bytes.
.SH BUGS
Native builds use
.I output.o
as an intermediate object filename.
An existing file at that name can be overwritten and removed.
Concurrent builds to the same output are not safe.
.PP
Some driver I/O failures still cause a Rust panic, usually exiting 101,
rather than a normal diagnostic.
These include object-file writes, starting the linker, and stdout writes
to a broken pipe or full device.
A failed completion message can occur after an executable has been created.
.PP
Regex anchors have known bugs around internal newlines and global-match
resumption.
They are not reliable whole-subject validation boundaries.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ld (1)
.PP
The project README covers building and first use;
.I docs/GUIDE.md
describes the compiler architecture.
.PP
.UR https://github.com/dan4thewin/safebash
.UE
